// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
// Donor: rustc `method::probe` + `AssocItem.fn_has_self_parameter` ("the
// receiver is the first parameter, resolve by receiver type"), separate chaining
// (name -> first row + `next_row`, TAOCP 6.4) for the table itself, rustc
// `report_method_error` / clang `Sema::NoteAllOverloadCandidates` ("a refusal
// names the whole candidate set"), and Nova's own rule P14 (10-overloading.md)
// for selection by MODE. NOT taken: a uniform DefId (a payload here is a ROW),
// inference of any kind (E2-b3), and any tie-break -- an ambiguous set is
// refused, never resolved by an order nobody declared.
// Role: layer 3 -- THE callable registry and its resolution: which callable does this call name.
// Used by: check (resolution and its refusals), emit_c and mono (the row a declaration owns), sem itself.
// Guarded by: check-novac-row-fields.py (row fields declared in plan 10.3v), check-novac-no-alloc-in-lookup.py (a door
// allocates nothing), check-novac-file-size.py (this file exists because sem.nv crossed 1000 lines), the module tests
// of mangle and pipeline.
// novac/src/sem/callables.nv -- the callable registry: rows, the name door, and
// the rule that picks one.
//
// A co-equal file of the `sem` module, cut along MEANING: "what is declared in
// this file" (sem.nv) and "which callable does a CALL name" are two questions,
// and the second one grew a rule of its own on 2026-08-21 -- the mode axis.
//
// THE RULE, in one place: a call fits a row when the FORM matches (a free call
// cannot reach a callable declared with a receiver), the arity matches, every
// typed position matches, and every position's MODE admits the argument's
// CLASS. Among the rows that fit, the most SPECIFIC wins (consume beats mut
// beats ro); a tie is an overload set and is refused rather than chosen.

module novac.sem

import ../tree.{Node}
import ../diag.{ice}
import ../names.{NameTable, NamespaceId}
import ../builtins.{ENTRY_FN}
import ../types.{TyId, raw_ty, no_ty, is_ty}

/// A ROW of the callable registry -- and a TYPE, not a bare `int`, since
/// 2026-08-21 (plan 274 section 9.1g; the owner's decision of 2026-08-20).
///
/// Three index spaces used to share one `int`: a row of this table, a type id,
/// and an interner term. Nothing in the compiler told them apart, and they
/// diverged silently -- this week the emitter asked `defs` for a row BY NAME
/// (last-write-wins) and got another overload's row, so two declarations emitted
/// under one C name and clang refused the unit. A newtype makes that a compile
/// error at the door instead of a defect at the link step.
///
/// `-1` still means "no row" in the STORAGE (a dense int vector), and the doors
/// answer `Option[FnRow]` -- the representation is a choice of this file, the
/// language of a door is the language of the caller.
export type FnRow int

/// The row's index, for the ONE thing a row is for: addressing a vector.
///
/// A free function and not a method, deliberately: novac compiles no method
/// BODY yet, and its own source must stay inside what it can read (the whole
/// reason section 9.1g waited for the newtype form to arrive first). The cast
/// inside is the language's unwrap, and novac reads exactly that direction --
/// newtype into its representation -- since the same day.
export fn raw_row(r FnRow) -> int => r as int

/// "No row", as a ROW rather than as a number the caller must recognise.
///
/// The storage keeps `-1` (a dense int vector cannot hold an absence any other
/// way), and every door that can fail answers `Option[FnRow]`. This is for the
/// one place in between: a walk that has not found a candidate yet.
export fn no_row() -> FnRow => FnRow(-1)

/// How a callable lookup turned out. A sum, not an int code: "not found"
/// and "more than one candidate" are different answers, and both must be
/// representable (П13.5; a silent int default is the №652 class).
///
/// EVERY variant carries a ROW, never a type id (owner's review 2026-08-16).
/// Two reasons. First, the pair (receiver, name) does NOT determine a return
/// type while the language overloads on PARAMETERS: two callables can share
/// the pair and differ in their parameters, so a door that answered with a
/// type would be claiming more than it can know. It answers with the
/// candidate instead, and the caller reads `ret_id` off the row once the
/// candidate is settled. Second, a row gives the caller the parameters too,
/// which is exactly what choosing between overloads needs.
///
/// `FnOverloaded` is not "ambiguous": it means the set has more than one
/// member and picking needs the ARGUMENT types, which `check` will have when
/// it gets local typing (274.3 wave 3, finding C9). Today novac refuses such
/// a call honestly rather than guessing. The payload is the FIRST row of the
/// set — naming the whole set needs a door this file does not have yet (see the
/// allocate; the door itself stays allocation-free (П18).
export type FnLookup enum
    | FnFound(FnRow)
    | FnUnknown
    | FnOverloaded(FnRow)

/// One callable — plan 273 form: a method IS a function whose first
/// parameter is the receiver, so there is ONE row shape for both.
///
/// The receiver is NOT a field of this row (owner's check 2026-08-16: "in
/// plan 273 the receiver is a parameter like all the others — are you sure
/// you should be matching on it?"). It is `params[param_off]` of a method,
/// and the row says only WHETHER the first parameter is a receiver. The
/// earlier shape kept `recv_id` and stored no other parameter at all:
/// `harvest_fn` validated the parameter types and then threw them away, so
/// the receiver was not "a parameter like the others" — it was the only
/// parameter novac remembered. Keeping them all is also what overload
/// resolution needs later; today it is what makes the form honest.
export type FnDef value {
    name str /// name as written in the declaration
    ret_id TyId /// return type id; `no_ty()` = none declared (legal for main only)
    param_off int /// first parameter of this callable in FnTable.params
    param_cnt int /// how many parameters, INCLUDING the receiver when there is one
    has_recv bool /// the first parameter IS the receiver — the ONE datum here (see below)
    tparam_off int /// start of this callable's type parameters in FnTable.tparams
    tparam_cnt int /// how many type parameters it declares; 0 = not generic
    next_row FnRow /// next row carrying the SAME name, or `FnRow(-1)`; see FnTable
}

/// IS THIS ROW THE PROGRAM'S ENTRY POINT? Asked of the ROW, never of the
/// spelling -- and that distinction is a fix, not a nicety.
///
/// The entry point is special for a reason that has nothing to do with novac:
/// its C symbol is dictated from OUTSIDE. The shell the oracle emits calls
/// `nova_fn_main_impl()`, so that one name cannot carry parameter types the way
/// every other name of ours does (274 section 7.0), it may declare no return
/// type, it is the root of the reachability walk, and its body gets a `(void)`
/// signature. Four exemptions, all legitimate, all keyed on ONE question.
///
/// Until 2026-08-22 that question was `fd.name == ENTRY_FN`, in six places
/// across four modules -- so EVERY callable spelled `main` inherited all four
/// exemptions. Measured on two files the oracle accepts:
///
///   * `fn main(x int) -> int` beside `fn main()`: `typing` skipped binding the
///     parameters (they belong to "the entry point", which takes none), so the
///     body refused its own parameter with "unknown name: nothing with this name
///     is bound at this point" -- a refusal about a bound name;
///   * both rows would have been mangled to `nova_fn_main_impl`: two C functions
///     with one name, the exact collision closed on 2026-08-19 by putting
///     parameter types INTO the name, reopened here through the one name that
///     has no parameter types.
///
/// So the rule is three facts about the row and not one about the string: the
/// spelling, no parameters, no receiver. An overload of `main` is an ORDINARY
/// callable -- Nova allows it, the oracle compiles it, and it gets an ordinary
/// mangled name.
#realtime nogc
export fn is_entry_fn(fd FnDef) -> bool =>
    fd.name == ENTRY_FN && fd.param_cnt == 0 && !fd.has_recv

/// The same question by ROW, for the readers that hold one.
#realtime nogc
export fn FnTable @is_entry_row(row FnRow) -> bool
    requires raw_row(row) >= 0 && raw_row(row) < @rows.len(), "sem: is_entry_row of a row outside the registry"
{
    is_entry_fn(@rows[raw_row(row)])
}

/// THE entry row of this file, or None -- the root of the reachability walk.
/// Walks the name chain rather than trusting `defs`, which is keyed by the bare
/// name and answers with the LAST row of it: with an overload of `main` present,
/// "the row named main" is not the same question as "the entry point".
#realtime nogc
export fn FnTable @entry_row() -> Option[FnRow] {
    mut r = @heads.find(ENTRY_FN)
    while r >= 0 {
        ro row = FnRow(r)
        r = raw_row(@rows[r].next_row)
        if @is_entry_row(row) { return Some(row) }
    }
    None
}

/// WHERE THE RECEIVER MARK LIVES, and why it is one bit on the callable
/// rather than a flag on each parameter (owner, 2026-08-16, twice).
///
/// First reading: a bool on the function looked like a privileged fact about
/// one element stored on the container, so the mark moved onto the parameter
/// (`ParamDef.is_recv`). That was worse, and the owner said why: a per
/// parameter flag ADMITS STATES THE LANGUAGE FORBIDS — "the third parameter
/// is the receiver", "two receivers" — and the invariant then rests on
/// discipline instead of on construction.
///
/// The resolution: the POSITION is not data. Plan 273 §4.4 fixes it — "the
/// receiver is the first parameter, on the first step without exceptions" —
/// so position is a rule of the language, not a fact of this file. The only
/// datum left is WHETHER a receiver is there, which is a zero-or-one fact
/// about the CALLABLE. One bool on FnDef expresses exactly that and nothing
/// illegal: there is no way to say "the third one". Parameters go back to
/// being plain type ids, and the receiver is `params[param_off]` when
/// `has_recv` — a fact of the DECLARATION: the author wrote this callable with
/// a receiver. Resolution does not read it and must not: a method IS a function
/// whose first parameter is the receiver (plan 273 §4.4), so `@fits` compares
/// positions and nothing else (owner, 2026-08-19). Kept because it is not
/// derivable from the parameter list, and a diagnostic that wants to say "this
/// is a method, call it as `x.f()`" has nowhere else to read it from.

/// THE callable registry: file functions and the method signatures READ
/// from std declarations live in the SAME rows (П5: std names are data,
/// not compiler knowledge).
///
/// The door takes the NAME only, and rows sharing a name form a chain
/// through `next_row`; the receiver is compared as an INTEGER while walking
/// it. The first shape built the key as text ("${recv}.${name}") — the
/// owner's question 2026-08-16 ("what if there is no receiver?") holds,
/// since a free function keys as -1 and type ids are never negative, but the
/// shape was wrong for two better reasons: it allocated and formatted a
/// string on EVERY lookup, in the hottest path there is (П14), and it
/// encoded structure into text right after the architecture spent a whole
/// rule on "identity is not a name" (§4а). A chain costs one integer compare
/// per candidate and allocates nothing; chains are 1-3 long because they
/// only hold callables that literally share a name.
///
/// EVERY declaration gets its own row, including a second one for the same
/// (receiver, name). The first shape kept a `dup` flag on the winner and
/// DROPPED the loser — so the compiler knew an overload existed but could no
/// longer say which signatures collided, and no diagnostic could ever name
/// them (owner's review 2026-08-16). Ambiguity is now a property of the
/// chain, not a flag on a row.
export type FnTable {
    tparams []str /// type-parameter NAMES of every callable, grouped by tparam_off/cnt
    rows []FnDef /// every callable, in registration order; a row index is what `DefTarget.DefFn` carries
    params []ParamDef /// parameters of every callable -- type AND mode in one row, addressed by FnDef.param_off/param_cnt
    heads NameTable /// name -> FIRST row with that name; `FnDef.next_row` walks the rest
    own_cnt int /// how many LEADING rows this file declared; the rest are signatures read from declarations
}

/// An empty callable registry.
export fn FnTable.new() -> FnTable =>
    { rows: []FnDef.new(), params: []ParamDef.new(), heads: NameTable.new(NamespaceId.NsCallables),
      tparams: []str.new(), own_cnt: 0 }

/// Does novac compile this callable's BODY?
///
/// The file's own callables are registered by `collect_types`, the signatures of
/// std methods by `harvest_methods` AFTER it, so the file's rows are a PREFIX
/// and the boundary is ONE number. A bit per row would admit "the third row is
/// theirs, the fourth is ours" -- a state that cannot occur; the same argument
/// put the receiver mark on the callable instead of on each parameter (§10.3в).
/// Nor is this a derived field on a ROW (П22): it is a fact about the TABLE,
/// like its length, answered in O(1).
///
/// WHY CHECK ASKS AT ALL. Every C name is ours now (owner, 2026-08-20: there is
/// no `foreign` in that sense -- Nova's external declarations are
/// `extern "C" fn` / `extern "nova" fn`, and they say so IN THE SOURCE). A call
/// to a body novac does not emit therefore names a symbol that exists nowhere,
/// so the subset declines it by name instead of emitting C that cannot link.
#realtime nogc
export fn FnTable @compiles_body(row FnRow) -> bool => raw_row(row) < @own_cnt

/// Does this row take a call with these argument types?
///
/// ONE rule for every callable: the arity first, then each parameter against
/// the argument standing in the same position. A receiver is position ZERO and
/// nothing else — plan 273 §4.4 fixes it there — so `x.f(a)` asks with the
/// types of `[x, a]` exactly as `f(x, a)` would. There is no receiver-shaped
/// question anywhere in this table any more: it was the last one (owner,
/// 2026-08-19, after the same correction landed in the mangling).
///
/// A position the caller cannot type yet is passed as -1 and fits anything.
/// `check` still lacks local types in some places (274.3/C9), and refusing
/// what it cannot judge would be a guess in the other direction. ARITY,
/// however, is always known at a call site, so it discriminates from today.
#realtime nogc
fn FnTable @fits(row FnRow, args []ArgSpec, form CallForm) -> bool {
    ro fd = @rows[raw_row(row)]
    // THE FORM FIRST. A free call cannot reach a callable declared with a
    // receiver, and a method call cannot reach one declared without: the
    // language keeps the two spellings apart, so resolution does too. Without
    // this an arity coincidence was enough -- `byte_len("abc")` found
    // `str @byte_len()` (probe 2026-08-20).
    ro wants_recv = match form {
        FreeCall => false
        MethodCall => true
    }
    if fd.has_recv != wants_recv {
        return false
    } else {
        // The declaration was written in the form the call site uses.
    }
    if fd.param_cnt != args.len() {
        return false
    } else {
        // Same arity: the positions can be compared one by one below.
    }
    for i in 0..fd.param_cnt {
        ro p = @params[fd.param_off + i]
        if args[i].type_id >= 0 && p.type_id != args[i].type_id {
            return false
        } else {
            // Either this position is not typed yet (no_ty()), or it matches.
        }
        // THE MODE, by rule P14. A parameter's mode is an axis of overloading,
        // not decoration: `tag(r)` and `tag(m)` carry the same TYPE and select
        // different callables, so a resolution comparing types alone could not
        // tell them apart -- it answered "overloaded, not narrowed" and refused
        // both (measured 2026-08-21).
        if !mode_admits(p.mode, args[i].cls) {
            return false
        } else {
            // This argument's class is one this mode accepts.
        }
    }
    true
}

/// How specific this row is: the sum of its parameters' mode specificities.
///
/// P14's filter 4 picks the most specific FITTING candidate, and for a set that
/// varies ONE parameter's mode the winner is unique by construction. A TIE is
/// not broken here -- `lookup` reports it as an overload set, because a set
/// varying two parameters oppositely has no winner and the spec says to refuse
/// rather than choose.
#realtime nogc
fn FnTable @specificity_of(row FnRow) -> int {
    ro fd = @rows[raw_row(row)]
    mut n = 0
    for i in 0..fd.param_cnt {
        n += @params[fd.param_off + i].mode.specificity()
    }
    n
}

/// Which substitution slot this name binds in this callable, or None when the
/// name is not one of its type parameters.
///
/// A type parameter is a BINDER, and its identity inside the declaration is its
/// NAME — the same reading `Scope.bind(name, type)` gives a local. The index is
/// the slot: `fn f[T, U]` binds T to 0 and U to 1, and a call's type arguments
/// stand in the same order (plan 274 E2-b2: turbofish is mandatory, so the
/// order is the whole mapping).
#realtime nogc
export fn FnTable @tparam_index(row FnRow, name str) -> Option[int] {
    ro fd = @rows[raw_row(row)]
    for i in 0..fd.tparam_cnt {
        // BINDER-OK: a type parameter is a binder of THIS declaration, so the
        // set is bounded by its own arity, not by the registry.
        if @tparams[fd.tparam_off + i] == name {
            return Some(i)
        } else {
            // Not this slot; the next one may bind the name.
        }
    }
    None
}


/// Register one callable. A repeat (receiver, name) is NOT merged and NOT
/// dropped: it becomes its own row in the same chain, so the collision stays
/// describable. Silent choosing between overloads is not allowed anywhere.
/// `param_ids` carries the receiver FIRST when `has_recv` — the plan 273
/// order (§4.4 fixes the position, so the caller has no other place to put
/// it, and no way to name a second one).
export fn FnTable mut @add(name str, ret_id TyId, has_recv bool, param_ids []ParamDef,
                          tparam_names []str) -> () {
    ro head = FnRow(@heads.find(name))
    ro off = @params.len()
    // Whole slice at once: std does the copy (nv-coding-style section 18a --
    // a counted push loop is the red flag it names).
    @params.append(param_ids)
    @heads.put(name, @rows.len())
    ro toff = @tparams.len()
    @tparams.append(tparam_names)
    @rows.push(FnDef { name, ret_id, param_off: off, param_cnt: param_ids.len(), has_recv,
                       tparam_off: toff, tparam_cnt: tparam_names.len(), next_row: head })
}

/// Whether a callable with this name exists at all.
///
/// The weakest question there is, and deliberately so: `check` walks the tree
/// before local typing, so at that point it has neither the receiver's type nor
/// the arguments'. A typo in the NAME is caught here; WHICH callable runs is
/// settled later by `@lookup`, which asks with types.
///
/// Until 2026-08-19 this answered only about names that had a receiver — a
/// receiver-shaped question in a form that has no receivers. Under plan 273 a
/// method IS a function whose first parameter is the receiver, so "is there a
/// callable called this" is the whole question, and `x.f()` failing to match
/// any row is reported by the typed pass, not guessed here.
#realtime nogc
export fn FnTable @has_name(name str) -> bool {
    @heads.find(name) >= 0
}

/// The candidate row for this call — or why there is none to name.
///
/// ONE DOOR, and it knows nothing about receivers: `x.f(a)` asks with the types
/// of `[x, a]`, `f(a)` asks with `[a]`, and both take the same path through the
/// same code. Counts the chain instead of trusting a flag: more than one row
/// fitting one call is an overload SET, and its first row is handed back so the
/// caller can enumerate the set without searching again.
/// Several rows fitting one call is NOT an overload set by itself: since
/// 2026-08-21 the modes decide, and `f(x T)` beside `f(mut x T)` fits a mut
/// argument BOTH ways. The winner is the most specific one (P14 filter 4), and
/// only a TIE at the top is a set -- which is what the spec says to refuse.
#realtime nogc
export fn FnTable @lookup(name str, args []ArgSpec, form CallForm) -> FnLookup {
    mut first = no_row()
    mut best = -1
    mut ties = 0
    mut r = @heads.find(name)
    while r >= 0 {
        ro row = FnRow(r)
        r = raw_row(@rows[r].next_row)
        if !@fits(row, args, form) { continue }
        ro s = @specificity_of(row)
        if raw_row(first) < 0 {
            first = row
        } else {
            // A later member of the same chain: the FIRST fitting row is what
            // a future candidate-set door would walk on from, so it is kept once.
        }
        if s > best {
            best = s
            ties = 1
        } else if s == best {
            ties += 1
        } else {
            // Less specific than one already seen: it loses by the rule, and
            // that is a DECISION, not a skip.
        }
    }
    if raw_row(first) < 0 { return FnLookup.FnUnknown }
    if ties > 1 {
        // Two candidates equally specific -- the set varies more than one
        // parameter's mode, and P14 refuses rather than choosing. The caller
        // enumerates the set from `first`.
        return FnLookup.FnOverloaded(first)
    } else {
        // One winner at the top of the specificity order.
    }
    FnLookup.FnFound(@most_specific(name, args, form, best))
}

/// The row with this specificity among the fitting candidates of `name`.
///
/// A second walk of a chain that is one to three rows long, and it buys the
/// property that matters: `lookup` hands back the WINNER, not the first row
/// that fitted. Doing it in one pass would mean remembering both the first row
/// and the best row and then explaining which is which at every call site.
#realtime nogc
fn FnTable @most_specific(name str, args []ArgSpec, form CallForm, want int) -> FnRow {
    mut r = @heads.find(name)
    while r >= 0 {
        ro row = FnRow(r)
        r = raw_row(@rows[r].next_row)
        if !@fits(row, args, form) { continue }
        if @specificity_of(row) == want { return row }
    }
    // The caller established that a row of this specificity fits; the chain is
    // walked with the same predicate, so failing to find it twice is a broken
    // invariant.
    ice("sem: the most specific candidate vanished between two walks of one chain")
}

// `candidates_from` lived here until 2026-08-21 and is gone with TWO reasons,
// both found because wrapping the type-id space made me read every door of this
// file. It was EXPORTED and imported by three files -- and CALLED BY NOBODY.
// And it was WRONG: it advanced the chain cursor and then pushed the ADVANCED
// value, so the "candidate set" it built held each candidate's SUCCESSOR and,
// on the last one, -1. Nothing ever printed a wrong list only because nothing
// ever printed a list. A door with no caller is deleted, not typed: handing
// `[]FnRow` to dead code would have made the defect look reviewed. When a
// refusal really needs to name the whole candidate set (D420; rustc lists the
// candidate impls, clang the candidate functions), it comes back with a test
// that reads the rows back.

